Class Structure

CLASS Task
    PROPERTIES:
        taskId
        taskName
        category
        completed
        dueDate
        createdAt

    METHODS:
        markComplete()
            set completed to true

        markIncomplete()
            set completed to false

        toggleComplete()
            IF completed is true THEN
                CALL markIncomplete()
            ELSE
                CALL markComplete()
            ENDIF
END CLASS

Task groups related data — name, category, due date, completion status, creation date — into one object instead of using five separate variables. This means each task moves, updates, and gets passed around as a single unit, rather than as scattered pieces that could get out of sync with each other.


CLASS Booking
    PROPERTIES:
        bookingId
        learnerName
        sessionType
        preferredDay
        preferredTime
        month
        notes
        status
 
    METHODS:
        confirm()
            Set status to "Confirmed"
 
        decline()
            Set status to "Declined"
END CLASS

Booking bundles the learner's request details and status together, and gives it its own confirm() and decline() methods. This keeps the assessor's action logic attached to the data it changes, rather than existing as a disconnected function operating on loose variables elsewhere in the program.month is included because the assessor dashboard calculates "Sessions This Month" by filtering bookings against this field — without it, that statistic has no data to read from.

CLASS Learner
    PROPERTIES:
        learnerName
        courseLevel
        tasks (list of Task objects)
        bookings (list of Booking objects)
        themePreference   - NEW ("dark" or "light")
END CLASS

A Learner can have many Tasks and many Bookings — a one-to-many relationship. Storing them as lists inside Learner reflects how the data is actually used in the app: everything shown on a learner's dashboard belongs to that one learner, so grouping it this way avoids having to search through unrelated records to find out which tasks or bookings belong to whom. courseLevel is included because it appears alongside the learner's name on both the Bookings page (assessor view) and the task list, so it belongs to the Learner rather than being repeated inside every Task or Booking record.

CLASS Assessor                    
    PROPERTIES:
        assessorName
        assignedLearners (list of Learner objects)
END CLASS


The dashboard is entirely from the assessor's point of view — their name are displayed, and everything shown (bookings, learners needing attention) belongs to them. Giving Assessor its own class, structured the same way as Learner, keeps staff-side data separate from learner-side data instead of mixing the two together.